 First, we need to convert the equation $x - 2y = 5$ into the slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$. To do that, we solve for $y$: \[
x - 2y = 5 \implies -2y = -x + 5 \implies y = \frac{1}{2}x - \frac{5}{2}
\] From this, we see that the $y$-intercept $b$ is $-\frac{5}{2}$. </think> <answer>$-\frac{5}{2}$</answer>

 The determinant of the matrix can be found by expanding along the first row:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} 0 & b - a & c - a \\ a - b & 0 & c - b \\ a - c & b - c & 0 \end{vmatrix}
&= 0\cdot\begin{vmatrix} 0 & c - b \\ b - c & 0 \end{vmatrix} 
- (b - a) \begin{vmatrix} a - b & c - b \\ a - c & 0 \end{vmatrix} 
+ (c - a) \begin{vmatrix} a - b & 0 \\ a - c & b - c \end{vmatrix} \\
&= (b - a)[(a - b)(0) - (c - b)(a - c)] 
- (c - a)[(a - b)(b - c) - 0(a - c)] \\
&= (b - a)[-(c - b)(a - c)] - (c - a)(a - b)(b - c) \\
&= (b - a)(a - c)(b - c) - (c - a)(a - b)(b - c) \\
&= (a - b)(b - c)(c - a) + (a - b)(b - c)(c - a) \\
&= 2(a - b)(b - c)(c - a).
\end{think}
<answer>$2 \left( a - b \right) \left( b - c \right) \left( c - a \right)$</answer>

